Maria's power bank started at an initial charge percentage of \(P\%\). After 5 hours of losing 9% of the total capacity each hour, the charge became \(P \times (0.91)^5\%\). After an additional 3 hours of losing 7% of the total capacity each hour, the charge became \(P \times (0.91)^5 \times (0.93)^3\%\). This final charge is given as 28%.

The equation is:
\[
P \times (0.91)^5 \times (0.93)^3 = 28
\]

Calculate the values:
- \((0.91)^5 \approx 0.624032\)
- \((0.93)^3 \approx 0.804357\)
- Product: \(0.624032 \times 0.804357 \approx 0.501945\)

Solve for \(P\):
\[
P = \frac{28}{0.501945} \approx 55.783
\]

Thus, the initial charge was approximately 55.783%. Since the charge is typically reported as a whole number in such contexts, it rounds to 56%.

